::ALG2_U02_L05_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = (x - 3)<sup>2</sup>(x + 1), where does the graph touch the x-axis? {
=x = 3 #Correct. x = 3 has even multiplicity.
~x = -1 #x = -1 has odd multiplicity, so the graph crosses there.
~x = 0 #x = 0 is not a zero of this polynomial.
~x = -3 #The sign is reversed; x - 3 = 0 gives x = 3.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = (x + 4)(x - 2), which zeros are shown? {
~x = 4 and x = -2 #The signs are reversed.
~x = -4 and x = -2 #Only x = -4 is correct.
=x = -4 and x = 2 #Correct. Solving x + 4 = 0 and x - 2 = 0 gives the zeros that anchor the sketch.
~x = 4 and x = 2 #Only x = 2 is correct.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What should be checked before sketching end behavior? {
~constant term only #The constant term does not control end behavior.
=degree and leading coefficient #Correct. The leading term uses both degree and leading coefficient to show how the graph behaves on the far left and far right.
~y-intercept only #The y-intercept is local information.
~middle coefficient only #Middle terms do not dominate end behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What usually happens at a zero with odd multiplicity? {
~the graph only touches #This is even multiplicity behavior.
~the graph disappears #Zeros remain on the graph.
~the graph becomes linear #Multiplicity does not make the function linear.
=the graph crosses #Correct. Odd multiplicity usually means the sign changes at the zero, so the graph crosses the x-axis.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = -x<sup>4</sup> + 1, what happens at both ends? {
~both ends rise #That would require a positive leading coefficient.
=both ends fall #Correct. Even degree with negative leading coefficient falls on both ends.
~left falls and right rises #That is positive odd-degree behavior.
~left rises and right falls #That is negative odd-degree behavior.
}


